00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.980 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  thank you all for joining us my name is  Mike Connolly I'm a new state  representative for cambridge and  somerville thank you  [Applause]  last year I was one of just two  candidates to actually defeat an  incumbent for a seat in the state  legislature and I did it with the help  of mass piece action so thank you for  your support I just want to talk a  little bit about you know what called me  to public service what led to my getting  involved with public service and how the  topic under discussion today around  ending nuclear proliferation and really  ending militarism more broadly is so  essential to everything else that I'm  trying to accomplish  in the state legislature so I was born  in Dorchester my parents divorced when I  was about five my mother and I moved to  public housing in Norwood Massachusetts  I benefited from things like head start  as a kid but as you can see I'm a giant  I was good on the football field I got a  full athletic scholarship to Duke  University and then after that I came  back to the BC law became an attorney  and along the way you know growing up in  public housing it was instilled in me  really that the Democratic Party would  be the party that would fight for anyone  who faced a difficulty in life but as  the years went by and as I saw the  profound wealth and income inequality in  our world as I looked at you know the  influence of money on our political  system I really thought it was important  that we as Democrats and we as people on  the Left do a lot more to engage our  political system and try to make sure  that our party and our government live  up to those values that really sustains  me when I was growing up so that was  really you know what called me into  public service I had an opportunity to  work on things like affordable housing  and sustainability here in Cambridge and  that all culminated with  a huge grassroots campaign there were  literally hundreds of volunteers in our  community who supported me in various  ways knocking on doors making phone  calls to help us win our election last  year the gentleman I defeated had  actually won 38 elections in a row prior  to our victory so it was it was a  shocker  for some so thank you all in the room  who helped us on this struggle so now  I'm at the Statehouse and I'm fighting  for a bold progressive agenda I'm proud  to say that I would call it a democratic  socialist agenda I'm fighting for a $15  minimum wage paid family medical leave  single-payer health care Medicare for  all Thank You congresswoman fair  applause on that I'm fighting for  affordable housing I think that this  idea that we had of public housing in  our country we're kind of losing it I  think we should actually go back to  raising revenue and saying let's put  these funds into building the housing so  I'm fighting for public housing I'm  fighting for affordable housing I'm  fighting for criminal justice reform 2nz  yeah thank you  to end the to end the racist war on  drugs and to end mandatory minimum  sentencing which I'm sorry to say we  still have here in our Commonwealth to a  far too great of an extent and one final  thing I'm fighting for the Safe  Communities Act which would make us a  sanctuary state so that we don't lose  sight of the fact that we're a nation of  immigrants so those are some of the  things I'm fighting for but you know I  think it really struck me when I saw the  flier for the event and how this event  coincides with the 50th anniversary of  dr. Martin Luther King's beyond Vietnam  Street speech which is also known as a  time to break the silence and in that  speech dr. King said how difficult it  really is for any of us whether we're in  government or whether we're just you  know activists acting on our own as  citizens how difficult it really is to  and up to the war machine to stand up to  the military into the these imperialist  notions that have always been a part of  our country you know there are some  people who said that when dr. King gave  that speech It was as if he was signing  his own death warrant and he faced a lot  of criticism for giving that speech but  I just want to quote one paragraph  because I think it's so important to  what we're talking about today he said  I'm convinced that if we are to get on  the right side of the world revolution  we as a nation must undergo a radical  revolution of values we must rapidly  begin to shift from a thing oriented  society to a person oriented society he  said that when machines and computers  profit motives and property rights are  considered more important than people  than the giant Triplets of racism  materialism and militarism are incapable  of being conquered and it's you know  it's just amazing how those words are so  prophetic and how they they ring just as  true today forty-nine years later as  they did when he delivered that speech  in New York City on April 4th 1967 and  you know I think as I think about you  know these progressive movements that  I'm involved in and I think about the  campaign's that have been you know I  think that we've given some really good  voice those of us on the left to issues  of income and wealth inequality I was  part of the Occupy Wall Street movement  and there was a time where this idea is  inequality really you know really wasn't  on the radar and today it's on  everyone's radar even Donald Trump as a  candidate would talk about inequality  even though his policies would do  nothing but make it worse you know on  the topic of racism and white supremacy  we clearly have so much more work to do  but there are movements like black lives  matter that are engaging people in our  community and that are really elevating  this discussion in a positive way and it  gives us a path forward as we try to  continue to deal with the legacy of  racism and  can you to fight white supremacy that's  represented by Donald Trump and that is  all too common all over our country but  then when it comes to that third triplet  that third evil triplet that dr. King  spoke about forty-nine years ago  militarism and imperialism I'm afraid  that you know those of us on the Left  have so much more work to do to give  voice to that concern for our generation  and so that's why thank you that's why  I'm so you know happy to be here be a  part of this so you know one of the ways  that we can really address you know the  role of military in our budget is this  people's budget campaign and we just had  a workshop a little while ago where we  talked a bit about the people's budget  it's really a road map for resistance  and it's powerful because it provides a  positive alternative it's not just  saying we are opposed to this we don't  want that it's saying here's what we  could have in particular if we you know  didn't commit some 54% of our  discretionary budget as Professor King  pointed out to the military if we were  able to dial that back to a more  reasonable posture what are all the  other things that we could do we could  have affordable housing we could have  health care as a human right we could  have pre-k just like I had a head start  program I ask why isn't it that every  child in our state and in our country  cannot have all of the early education  all of the childcare all of the public  education all the way through college  and grad school that they could possibly  need we could have all those things if  we adopted a piece of people's budget  I'll say a few words about our own state  budget  I'm glad we're joined by my colleague  rep Provo from Somerville and we work  very closely in the Statehouse really  trying to tackle issues in our own state  budget so there's a fact that I want to  share with you our state budget is about  3.5 billion dollars less today than it  was 17 years ago when you adjust for  inflation and that's not an anomaly  over the past two decades or so we have  under invested by about fifty billion  dollars here in our Commonwealth and  that is because we had a series of tax  cuts about 17 20 years ago on income tax  on dividends and interest on corporate  tax rates we cut taxes across the board  and we have lost tens of billions of  dollars of investment right here in  democratic Massachusetts where we have a  super majority of Democrats in our  legislature we still cannot find the  momentum or the willingness to actually  raise the revenue we need to fund the  MBTA to fund public housing and  affordable housing to fund all of the  things we need to continue to be a  Commonwealth that we all love and  appreciate thank you so you know I would  just you know stand here to say that you  know as a new state representative as  you know a member and a supporter of  this organization I would just you know  ask us all to continue to echo this call  that dr. King gave some 49 years ago and  to understand that to address any one of  these three giant evils whether it's the  militarism whether it's the materialism  or whether it is the racism to address  any one of these three evils it's going  to mean that we tackle all three head-on  because they're all interconnected and  it's going to be impossible for us to  really have the kind of progressive  society we want if we allow you know  this nuclear weapons or the this  military budget to continue the way it  has so that's what I'm looking forward  to do I'm really grateful to join all of  you today and I'm looking forward to  hearing the congresswoman speak soon and  I'm looking forward to many more fights  and victories in the months and years to  come so thank you for having me  okay  [Music]

